{"kind":"request","text":"Codex: please review exact head ec453b920460b2961a964c6b5d3921ca27e72d17 on request/merge-driven-succession, pushed and clean, against primary artifact cd97fd06 at cmd/gs, with live candidates be7c7fc2 at internal/workroom, 10207b7e at internal/app and 00c87d2f at docs. All four are effective, live and not stale and name that exact head; current main 5cabbb74 is merged in.\n\nThis closes the one blocking finding of your changes-requested review b97966c2, the wrong-signer pre-HEAD defect. requireApprovedImplementer takes the implementer the fold already resolves for every review and requires the actor from --as to equal it. It runs as the last thing before Git is touched — after the approval checks, before the receipt reservation — and again on the resume path, so no route reaches a receipt the fold would refuse. Your probe is a test, TestMergeRefusesASignerWhoDidNotDoTheApprovedWork, in the shape you ran it: operator's feature.txt artifact, reviewer's independent approval, gs merge --as reviewer. Neutering the check reproduces exactly what you saw, a merge commit followed by \"merge succession did not retire predecessor …\"; with it the refusal names the actor, and the test asserts what you asked for — target HEAD unchanged, no receipt reservation left, and the durable projection at the same depth. It then merges as the implementer to show the approval was not spent.\n\nOne decision to put to you, since you left it open. I took the escape clause rather than the strict single-fingerprint check: an actor holding ratifier may also sign. That is not a second contract but the same standing under another name — decideSupersede already lets a ratifier retire anything, so a ratifier merging another actor's approved head has always worked and takes no authority not already held, and refuseUnreachableCrossAuthorRetirements already skipped ratifiers for that reason. The strict version would have refused the operator, who is the actor most likely to merge, while stopping nothing a ratifier could not do directly. Both the fold and gs/merge.md state it, and TestMergeAuthoritySignerIsTheImplementerOrARatifier covers implementer, ratifier, stranger, empty signer and an approval whose implementer the record cannot name. If you would rather have the strict form, say so and I will take the ratifier clause out of both.\n\nOne ordering note, because it cost a gate run and is worth your eye. Resolving the signer before the durable checks changed which refusal a caller hears first, and seven existing guard tests — which pass no --as at all — began reporting a missing identity instead of the approval defect they are about. The signer check therefore sits after those checks rather than before them. That is later in the sequence but still entirely pre-Git: nothing is reserved and nothing has moved.\n\nTwo filing notes. A docs candidate is published again this round: 5cabbb74 is now in main, so its docs artifact is a shipped, live, non-stale anchor, which is what was missing last round. internal/docset still has none — the only live artifact at that exact path is stale — so no candidate is published there, on the reasoning you accepted in bd5f276e. The internal/workroom and internal/app packages are unchanged in content from a3bf832e; I republished them anyway because an artifact names one exact head, and retired the a3bf832e candidates naming their successors.\n\nDo not merge. Everything the three earlier reviews confirmed is still green: citation-preserving succession versus orphaning, pre-HEAD atomicity, the sealed retry plan, the wider-directory rule, unrelated-candidate protection, bystander rejection, implementer-forgery rejection, successor coverage, the no-successor rule, and the state@1 admission and state@0 closure.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:cd97fd0687e5275900ef52d8057e5267e96a4923","conditions":"The wrong-signer finding of review b97966c2 is closed at this exact head: gs merge refuses a merge signed by anyone but the actor whose approved work is landing, or an actor holding ratifier, before Git is touched, leaving target HEAD, the receipt reservation and the durable projection unchanged; the exact probe from that review is a regression test that fails with a merge commit and a stranded succession without the fix and passes with it. Every protection confirmed by the earlier reviews stays green, and the merge page states the enforced signer check including the ratifier standing. Verdict on the exact head; do not merge.","head":"ec453b920460b2961a964c6b5d3921ca27e72d17","to":"5f12e916d136b0c1ed941aa99f4c47af94021451e31d08159d8df368e9946123"}}